## Redis Data Structures

This is the most important thing to understand.

**Redis is NOT just key-value.** It has 5 data structures:

```
String  → single value
Hash    → like a Java Map (object fields)
List    → like a Java List (ordered)
Set     → unique values (no duplicates)
ZSet    → Set with score/ranking
```

Each has its own operations in RedisTemplate.

---

## 1. String Operations (opsForValue)

Most common. Store single value against a key.

```java
@Service
public class StringRedisService {

    @Autowired
    private RedisTemplate<String, Object> redisTemplate;

    public void stringExamples() {

        ValueOperations<String, Object> ops = 
                            redisTemplate.opsForValue();

        // SET - store value
        ops.set("username:1", "John");

        // SET with TTL
        ops.set("username:1", "John", Duration.ofMinutes(10));

        // GET - retrieve value
        String name = (String) ops.get("username:1");

        // SET only if key does NOT exist (used in locking!)
        Boolean created = ops.setIfAbsent("lock::key", "1",
                                         Duration.ofSeconds(10));

        // Increment counter
        ops.increment("visitCount");         // +1
        ops.increment("visitCount", 5);      // +5
        ops.decrement("visitCount");         // -1

        // Get and Set at same time
        String oldValue = (String) ops.getAndSet("username:1", "Jane");
        // returns "John", stores "Jane"
    }
}
```

Redis stores:
```
username:1   → "John"
visitCount   → 42
lock::key    → "1"  (TTL: 10 sec)
```

