## 2. Hash Operations (opsForHash)

Store object fields separately. Like a Map inside Redis.

```java
public void hashExamples() {

    HashOperations<String, String, Object> ops = 
                        redisTemplate.opsForHash();

    // Store individual fields
    ops.put("user:1", "name",   "John");
    ops.put("user:1", "email",  "john@gmail.com");
    ops.put("user:1", "salary", 95000);

    // Store entire map at once
    Map<String, Object> userMap = new HashMap<>();
    userMap.put("name",   "John");
    userMap.put("email",  "john@gmail.com");
    userMap.put("salary", 95000);
    ops.putAll("user:1", userMap);

    // Get single field
    String name = (String) ops.get("user:1", "name");

    // Get all fields
    Map<String, Object> user = ops.entries("user:1");

    // Get specific fields only
    List<Object> values = ops.multiGet("user:1",
                          List.of("name", "email"));

    // Update single field (no need to reload whole object!)
    ops.put("user:1", "salary", 100000);

    // Delete single field
    ops.delete("user:1", "email");

    // Check field exists
    Boolean exists = ops.hasKey("user:1", "name");
}
```

Redis stores:
```
user:1
  ├── name   → "John"
  ├── email  → "john@gmail.com"
  └── salary → 95000
```

### String vs Hash — When to use which?

```
String → store entire User object as JSON
Hash   → store each field separately

String:
user:1 → {"name":"John","email":"john@gmail.com","salary":95000}
→ To update salary, fetch whole object, change, store back

Hash:
user:1 → {name, email, salary}
→ To update salary, just ops.put("user:1", "salary", 100000)
→ Much more efficient! ✅
```

